/-!
# Cutting a Cohen-Macaulay fibre down by one, and the affine slice it produces

Fix a groupoid scheme `(U, R, s, t, c, e, i)` with `s` and `t` Cohen-Macaulay and locally of
finite presentation, and a finite type point `u` of `U`. Write `F_u = s⁻¹(u)` and let `G_u` be
the stabilizer fibre, so that `d₁ = dim(G_u)` and `d₂ = dim_{e(u)}(F_u)`. Stacks 0461 asserts,
when `d₂ > d₁`, the existence of an affine `U'` and a `g : U' ⟶ U` which is an immersion locally
of finite presentation whose image contains `u`, such that the morphism
`h : U' ×_{g,U,t} R ⟶ U` is Cohen-Macaulay at `(u, e(u))` and the restricted source fibre has
dimension one less than `d₂`.

This file contains five things: the ring-theoretic engine of that argument, the part of the
conclusion which is independent of the choice of the cutting parameter, the application of the
engine to the fibre `F_u` which the identification of `RestrictedArrowStalk.lean` makes
possible, Stacks 046Z in the form that application hands it to, and the point `(u', e(u))` of
`U' ×_{g,U,t} R` at which clauses (4) and (5) are asserted.

## The engine

The local ring `C = 𝒪_{F_u, e(u)}` is Cohen-Macaulay of dimension `d₂`, and the local ring of
the restriction of the groupoid to the residue field at `u` is the further quotient of `C` by
the ideal generated by the image of the maximal ideal under the target map; its dimension is
`d₁`. The engine takes exactly that data — a ring map `t : A →+* C` into a Cohen-Macaulay local
ring and an ideal `I` of `A` whose extension cuts the dimension strictly — and produces an
`f ∈ I` with three properties at once: `t f` is a nonzerodivisor on `C`, the quotient
`C ⧸ (t f)` is again Cohen-Macaulay, and its dimension is one less than that of `C`. The first
is the parameter choice of `PrimeAvoidance`, and the other two are Stacks 02JN read at the
one-element sequence `[t f]`.

The dimension drop is recorded additively, as `dim(C ⧸ (t f)) + 1 = dim(C)`, because Krull
dimensions take values in `WithBot ℕ∞`; this is how Stacks 02JN itself states it and it carries
the nondegeneracy `C ⧸ (t f) ≠ 0` that the truncated subtraction would lose.

## The slice

Cutting an affine chart around `u` by any section vanishing at `u` already produces an affine
scheme mapping to `U` by an immersion locally of finite presentation whose image contains `u`;
this is `exists_slice_of_mem_primeIdealOf` in `SliceChart.lean`. Those are clauses (1), (2) and
(3) of Stacks 0461, and they do not depend on which section is cut, so they hold with no
Cohen-Macaulayness or dimension hypothesis at all. They are recorded here in that generality.

Clauses (4) and (5) are *not* proved in this file; see the implementation notes for what is here
towards them and what is still missing.

## Main results

* `Stacks.CohenMacaulay.ParameterChoice.exists_mem_isSMulRegular_cohenMacaulay_quotient`: the
  engine — a parameter cutting a Cohen-Macaulay local ring down to a Cohen-Macaulay local ring
  of dimension one less.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.GroupoidScheme.exists_affine_slice_of_isFiniteTypePoint`: clauses (1), (2)
  and (3) of Stacks 0461.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.GroupoidScheme.exists_mem_maximalIdeal_isSMulRegular_cohenMacaulay_quotient`:
  the engine applied to the target map on the source fibre, its dimension hypothesis supplied by
  the identification of `RestrictedArrowStalk.lean`.
* `Module.Flat.isSMulRegular_and_flat_quotient_span_of_isSMulRegular_quotient` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.isSMulRegular_stalk_and_flat_quotient_of_isSMulRegular_fiberStalk`: Stacks
  046Z with its two quotients taken by ideals, and the same statement read at a point of a
  morphism of schemes through `quotientStalkEquivFiberStalk`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.SliceSituation.identityArrowPoint` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.SliceSituation.identityArrowFiberPoint`: the point `(u', e(u))` of
  `U' ×_{g,U,t} R` and the point of `h⁻¹(u)` it determines.

## Implementation notes

The engine is stated for a bare ring homomorphism `t : A →+* C` and an arbitrary ideal `I`,
matching `PrimeAvoidance`: the slicing argument applies it with `A` the ring of sections of an
affine chart of `U` around `u`, `I` the prime of that ring at `u`, and `C` the local ring of the
fibre `F_u` at `e(u)` — so neither locality of `t` nor Noetherianness of `A` is available, and
none is needed. Membership of `t f` in the maximal ideal of `C` is a hypothesis rather than a
consequence for the same reason.

Stacks 02JN binds the local ring structure on its quotients existentially and states them for
`Ideal.ofList (xs.take i)`. Both are transported here onto `Ideal.span {t f}` along
`Ideal.quotEquivOfEq`, which is why the Cohen-Macaulayness in the conclusion is the ring
predicate `IsCohenMacaulayLocalRing` under an existentially bound `IsLocalRing`: rewriting
`Ideal.ofList [t f]` to `Ideal.span {t f}` in place is not possible, the local ring instance
appearing in the statement depending on the ideal.

The engine is now applicable, because the local ring of the restricted arrow scheme at the
identity arrow is computed as `C ⧸ Ideal.map t (maximalIdeal 𝒪_{U,u})` in
`RestrictedArrowStalk.lean`; that is what connects
`ringKrullDim_stalk_restrictedArrows_eq_stabilizerFiber` to the engine's hypothesis and so
supplies `d₁`. The application is
`exists_mem_maximalIdeal_isSMulRegular_cohenMacaulay_quotient` above, with `A` the local ring of
`U` at `u` and `I` its maximal ideal.

Two of the three ingredients clauses (4) and (5) were missing are now here.

The point `(u', e(u))` of `U' ×_{g,U,t} R` is `SliceSituation.identityArrowPoint`. The scheme is
a fibre product, so the point is not available as a pair of points: it comes from the
surjectivity of the comparison of a fibre square of schemes onto the fibre product of the
underlying spaces, and the two projection equations in `SliceSituation.IdentityArrowPoint` are
all that is known about it. Those equations give `h (u', e(u)) = u`, hence the point
`SliceSituation.identityArrowFiberPoint` of the fibre `h⁻¹(u)` at which clause (4) is asserted.
That point is pinned by the equation `fiberι u (identityArrowFiberPoint D P) = P.point` rather
than left as a transport of `Scheme.Hom.asFiber`, which is the shape in which
`quotientStalkEquivFiberStalkAtPoint` consumes a point of a fibre.

Stacks 046Z is here in the form clause (4) needs it. Its module form takes the fibre as
`M ⧸ (Ideal.map (algebraMap R S) (maximalIdeal R) • (⊤ : Submodule S M))`, and at `M = S` that
submodule of `S` is the ideal `Ideal.map (algebraMap R S) (maximalIdeal R)` itself; so the
hypothesis and the conclusion are transported along `Submodule.quotEquivOfEq` and never compared
as types. The result is
`Module.Flat.isSMulRegular_and_flat_quotient_span_of_isSMulRegular_quotient`, whose fibre ideal
is now the one `quotientStalkEquivFiberStalk` produces; reading it at a point of a morphism of
schemes gives `isSMulRegular_stalk_and_flat_quotient_of_isSMulRegular_fiberStalk`, which turns
the nonzerodivisor delivered by the engine into flatness of the sliced local ring over
`𝒪_{U, u}` — the first half of the Cohen-Macaulayness of `h` at `(u', e(u))`.

The parameter the engine produces lives in the maximal ideal of `𝒪_{U, u}`, while the slice is cut
by a section of an affine chart. `SliceParameterSection.lean` reconciles the two: an element of
that maximal ideal is a unit multiple of the germ of a section lying in the prime of `u`, so the
engine's whole conclusion holds for such a section, which is
`GroupoidScheme.exists_mem_primeIdealOf_isSMulRegular_cohenMacaulay_quotient` there.

What clauses (4) and (5) still require, which is not in the repository:

* a computation of the local ring of `U' ×_{g,U,t} R` at `(u', e(u))` as `𝒪_{R, e(u)} ⧸ (t f)`,
  and of the local ring of `h⁻¹(u)` there as `𝒪_{F_u, e(u)} ⧸ (t f)`. Neither follows from
  `pullback_stalk_isLocalization`, which presents the local ring of a fibre product only as a
  localization of a tensor product at an unnamed submonoid: the submonoid consists of units here
  because the projection to `R` is a closed immersion, and pinning that needs the ideal cutting
  out the base change, not the localization statement. Two of the three steps of each computation
  are now available: `AffineIdealStalk.lean` computes the local ring of a subscheme cut out over
  an affine open by an ideal `J` as the quotient of the ambient local ring by the germs of `J`,
  and `QuotientBaseChange.lean` identifies the base change of `Spec(A/I) ⟶ Spec(A)` along
  `Spec(B) ⟶ Spec(A)` with `Spec(B/IB) ⟶ Spec(B)`. What is missing is the passage between them:
  that `U' ×_{g,U,t} R` restricted over an affine chart `Spec(B) ⊆ R` carried into `Spec(A)` by
  the target map is that affine base change, which needs the fibre product along a morphism
  factoring through an open immersion. With the two computations in hand, clause (4) is
  `isSMulRegular_stalk_and_flat_quotient_of_isSMulRegular_fiberStalk` for the flatness and the
  engine for the Cohen-Macaulayness of the fibre; and
* for clause (5), transport of the result of clause (4) across
  `SliceSituation.sliceFiberComparison` — pinned only up to its two projections — followed by
  Stacks 02FX to descend to the local ring.

